From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:19:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media (ohos): Release AVPlayer when its player is dropped

The info callback of the OpenHarmony AVPlayer backend held the AVPlayer
wrapper that owns it, and the video sink, which holds the wrapper too,
so no AVPlayer was ever released: every media element leaked its
player, decoder and callbacks until the process exited. Let the
callback hold weak references instead, and release AVPlayer when the
player is dropped.

Releasing waits for the running callbacks and calls the info callback,
both of which lock the wrapper, so release AVPlayer without holding
that lock. Releasing also waits for a pending read, and a read can wait
for the media element to answer a SeekData event, which the element
ignores once it has dropped the player, so answer that read first.

Free the AVPlayer callbacks only after releasing AVPlayer: the info
callback used to be freed before releasing AVPlayer, which calls it. A
native image can still call its frame-available listener after
OH_NativeImage_Destroy returns, so give it the id of the listener in a
registry rather than a pointer to it. Don't destroy the native window
after the native image, which already destroys it.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: nothing observable from a test page changes; see Validation.

Validation: a HiHope DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0, API 26), headless release builds (`./mach build --ohos --headless-binary`) of ohos-main with patches 68 to 74, with and without this patch. A page with six video elements that each switch the source of a playing 14 MB video once, five of them 0 to 100 ms after a seek that needs new data, and six more video elements that load and are then dropped, followed by TestUtils.gc() (`--pref dom_testutils_enabled`): AVPlayer logs 18 creations; without this patch none is released, even after the GC; with it, the six switched ones are released at once and all 18 after the GC, and each switch takes 7 to 23 ms. `./mach test-wpt --ohos` of media-elements/loading-the-media-resource, media-elements/error-codes and the series' ten media tests (60 tests): with `--processes 1`, every test matches its expectation with SpiderMonkey and with QuickJS, with 72 creations and 66 (SpiderMonkey) or 67 (QuickJS) releases, the rest belonging to elements that were not collected yet when the run ended; with `--processes 2` (SpiderMonkey, five runs), every test matches its expectation in four runs, and in one, two video subtests of gc-unreferenced-media-element fail with VID_DEC_ERR from the board's decoder limit, as the previous patch describes. No run crashed. A first version also destroyed the native window after the native image; it crashed in DestoryNativeWindow once its player was dropped.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
